osx-gpu: radeonsi on a big-endian host, part one: Mesa's memory layout and the GPU's

Two kinds of memory. A buffer is host-order (vertex data, uniforms, index
and storage buffers, as the application wrote them): every vertex fetch is
open-coded and swapped per element size, the shaders swap what they load
(part two), and a buffer is not offered as a texture or image through a
format wider than bytes, which no sampler could swap. A texture keeps the
GPU's own little-endian layout, so the colour buffer's ENDIAN field is
never used (the r600-era table swapped 8_8_8_8 whole and reversed every
clear and readback), the sampler and the compute blits read and write every
format natively, and the host-order layout Mesa's format code expects
(u_format_table.c) exists only in the CPU's view, which part two swaps at
the staging boundary. Formats with 8-bit channels are the same bytes in
either layout, which is why the first frames needed none of this; packed
formats (5_6_5, 4_4_4_4, 2_10_10_10) were at first not offered to the
sampler and the state tracker converted them on the CPU. Since part six
(the little-endian listing of the bitmask formats) they are offered;
RDN_BE_PACKED=0 withholds them again.
Measured on the W8100, 2026-11-04.

